    {
      "type": "page",
      "title": "Three presentation roles",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "section",
          "title": "Measurement or name",
          "flow": [
            {
              "kind": "para",
              "text": "The surface is decided by SEMANTICS (the author’s `label`, falling back to a classifier only when unspecified). The FACE is decided by CONTENT. They are separate questions, so a measurement can be numerals or algebra and a name can be a symbol or a word without either answer leaking into the other."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "figure",
          "figure": "geometry",
          "title": "Three faces on one figure",
          "caption": "\"3.21 cm\" is a MEASUREMENT (surface, upright numerals). \"c\" is a SYMBOLIC name (maths face, no surface). \"adjacent side\" is a PROSE name (upright text, no surface) — a word is not a variable, and italicising it reads as a product of eight letters.",
          "objects": [
            {
              "type": "points",
              "from": "table",
              "rows": [
                [
                  "A",
                  0,
                  0
                ],
                [
                  "B",
                  4.2,
                  0
                ],
                [
                  "C",
                  0,
                  3.1
                ]
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "polygon",
              "vertices": [
                "A",
                "B",
                "C"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "rightAngle",
              "at": "A",
              "between": [
                "B",
                "C"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "sideLabel",
              "between": [
                "A",
                "B"
              ],
              "text": "adjacent side",
              "label": "name"
            },
            {
              "type": "sideLabel",
              "between": [
                "A",
                "C"
              ],
              "text": "c",
              "label": "name"
            },
            {
              "type": "sideLabel",
              "between": [
                "B",
                "C"
              ],
              "text": "3.21",
              "unit": "cm",
              "label": "measure"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "figure",
          "figure": "geometry",
          "title": "Explicit label overrides the classifier",
          "caption": "EXPLICIT INTENT WINS, in both directions and against the classifier. \"AB\" would be classified a name and is forced to a measurement; \"2x\" would be classified a quantity and is forced to a name. \"3x + 2y + 15\" is a long SYMBOLIC measure — the only string here long enough to overflow when sized with the wrong face’s metrics. \"radius\" is unlabelled prose, so the fallback classifier resolves it.",
          "objects": [
            {
              "type": "points",
              "from": "table",
              "rows": [
                [
                  "A",
                  -2,
                  -1.4
                ],
                [
                  "B",
                  2,
                  -1.4
                ],
                [
                  "C",
                  1.4,
                  1.6
                ],
                [
                  "D",
                  -1.8,
                  1.3
                ]
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "polygon",
              "vertices": [
                "A",
                "B",
                "C",
                "D"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "sideLabel",
              "between": [
                "A",
                "B"
              ],
              "text": "AB",
              "label": "measure"
            },
            {
              "type": "sideLabel",
              "between": [
                "B",
                "C"
              ],
              "text": "2x",
              "label": "name"
            },
            {
              "type": "sideLabel",
              "between": [
                "C",
                "D"
              ],
              "text": "radius"
            },
            {
              "type": "sideLabel",
              "between": [
                "D",
                "A"
              ],
              "text": "3x + 2y + 15",
              "label": "measure"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "figure",
          "figure": "geometry",
          "title": "A word the author declares a measurement",
          "caption": "`{text:\"hypotenuse\", label:\"measure\"}` is legal — the author says the word IS the measurement, so it takes the surface. It must still be UPRIGHT: content decides the face even inside a chip, and italicising a word reads as a product of its letters.",
          "objects": [
            {
              "type": "points",
              "from": "table",
              "rows": [
                [
                  "A",
                  0,
                  0
                ],
                [
                  "B",
                  3.6,
                  0
                ],
                [
                  "C",
                  0,
                  2.7
                ]
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "polygon",
              "vertices": [
